# Elowen Update — Complete Light & Dark Mode System

Update the existing Elowen website to add a polished, site-wide light and dark mode system.

This is an UPDATE to the existing website, not a rebuild. Preserve all existing pages, tools, calculator functionality, data storage/export/import behavior, responsive design, accessibility, GitHub Pages compatibility, and the established Elowen visual identity unless a change is specifically required below.

Do not replace working features or redesign Elowen into a different style just to implement theming.

## Main requirement

Elowen must have two complete visual themes:

- Light mode
- Dark mode

Add one accessible theme toggle in a consistent global location, preferably the site header, so it is available throughout the website.

The toggle must use proper vector/CSS icons, not emoji characters:

- Light mode: a clean sun icon
- Dark mode: a clean crescent moon icon with a simple croissant-like crescent silhouette

The dark-mode icon must look like a moon, not a food croissant and not an emoji.

Both icons should use the same visual language: simple line/shape geometry, professional proportions, consistent stroke weight, and styling that matches Elowen.

## Toggle behavior

The theme button must:

- Switch themes immediately without reloading the page
- Work on every Elowen page
- Be keyboard accessible
- Have a visible focus state
- Have an appropriate accessible name
- Be comfortable to use on mobile/touch devices
- Use subtle hover, active, and press feedback
- Never use emoji

When light mode is active, display the sun icon.

When dark mode is active, display the crescent moon icon.

The accessible label/title should communicate the action clearly. For example:

- In light mode: `Switch to dark mode`
- In dark mode: `Switch to light mode`

Do not depend on the icon alone for accessibility.

## Theme persistence

Persist the user's selected theme locally.

Requirements:

- Save the user's explicit theme choice in local storage or Elowen's existing preferences system
- Preserve it after page refreshes
- Preserve it while navigating between pages
- Preserve it after closing and reopening the browser when local storage remains available
- Do not require an account or server

If the user has never selected a theme, use the operating system/browser `prefers-color-scheme` preference for the initial theme.

Once the user manually chooses light or dark mode, that explicit choice takes priority over the system preference.

If Elowen already exports/imports preferences in its backup system, include the explicit theme preference in exported Elowen data and restore it during a valid import.

Do not break compatibility with existing saved Elowen data that does not contain a theme field.

## Prevent theme flash

Avoid a flash of the wrong theme during initial page loading.

Apply the saved or preferred theme as early as reasonably possible before the main interface is painted.

Do not wait until all page JavaScript has finished loading before deciding the initial theme if that creates an obvious light-to-dark or dark-to-light flash.

The implementation should remain simple and compatible with GitHub Pages.

## Light mode

Keep Elowen's existing warm, calm visual identity.

The light theme should continue to be based around the existing palette:

- Main UI: Warm Sand
- Interactive elements: Soft Taupe
- Dark areas/text: Desert Stone
- Press/active feedback: Very Light Beige
- Icons: Warm Brown-Gray

Refine exact shades if needed for accessibility or consistency, but do not replace the warm neutral identity with generic white/gray/blue styling.

The light theme should feel:

- Warm
- Minimal
- Soft
- Professional
- Calm
- Clear

Avoid harsh pure-white surfaces wherever a warmer neutral works better.

## Dark mode

Create a deliberate dark interpretation of Elowen rather than simply inverting the light theme.

The dark theme should remain warm and natural.

Use a palette direction such as:

- Main background: deep warm charcoal/stone
- Main surfaces: slightly lighter warm stone
- Elevated surfaces/cards: subtly lighter than the main surface
- Interactive elements: muted warm taupe
- Primary text: soft warm cream/sand
- Secondary text: muted beige-gray
- Icons: warm taupe/brown-gray
- Borders: restrained warm neutral borders
- Hover states: slightly lighter warm surfaces
- Press states: subtle warm beige/taupe feedback
- Focus rings: clearly visible and accessible without becoming neon

Avoid:

- Pure black backgrounds
- Generic blue-gray dark themes
- Cold navy backgrounds
- Neon colors
- Bright glowing borders
- Pure white everywhere
- Excessive contrast
- Heavy shadows
- Gradient-heavy styling

Dark mode should feel like the same Elowen product viewed at night, not a separate design.

## Use semantic theme variables

Implement theming primarily through reusable CSS custom properties.

Prefer semantic variables such as:

```css
:root {
  --color-bg: ...;
  --color-surface: ...;
  --color-surface-elevated: ...;
  --color-text: ...;
  --color-text-muted: ...;
  --color-border: ...;
  --color-interactive: ...;
  --color-interactive-hover: ...;
  --color-interactive-active: ...;
  --color-icon: ...;
  --color-focus: ...;
}

[data-theme="dark"] {
  /* Dark-theme overrides */
}
```

Use the project's existing naming system if one already exists and is well structured.

Do not duplicate entire stylesheets for light and dark mode.

Do not scatter dozens of unrelated hard-coded color values throughout components when a semantic variable can be used instead.

