## Accessibility

Both themes must be fully accessible.

Requirements:

- Maintain readable text contrast
- Maintain distinguishable borders and controls
- Keep keyboard focus highly visible
- Do not use color alone to communicate state
- The toggle must be a real button
- Decorative SVG details should be hidden from assistive technology where appropriate
- The control needs an accessible label
- Interactive targets must remain touch friendly

Do not reduce accessibility in dark mode to make it look softer.

## Motion

Theme transitions should be subtle and fast.

It is acceptable to softly transition properties such as:

- Background color
- Text color
- Border color
- Surface color
- Icon color

Do not animate every layout property.

Do not use dramatic fades, flashes, spins, bouncing, or large scaling effects when switching themes.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` and disable/reduce decorative transitions for users who request reduced motion.

## Icons

Use clean inline SVG or equivalent lightweight local vector implementation.

Sun icon requirements:

- Simple circular center
- Clean rays
- Balanced proportions
- No emoji

Dark-mode icon requirements:

- Crescent moon shape
- Smooth, recognizable crescent
- Visually comparable in size to the sun icon
- Croissant-like crescent silhouette only in the geometric sense
- Must still clearly read as a moon
- No face
- No stars unless extremely subtle and genuinely beneficial
- No emoji
- No cartoon food styling

The icon should inherit its color from CSS (`currentColor`) where practical so it themes correctly.

## Responsive behavior

The theme toggle must work cleanly on:

- Desktop
- Laptop
- Tablet
- Phone
- Portrait orientation
- Landscape orientation

Do not let the toggle cause navigation overflow or awkward wrapping.

Do not reduce the tap target to the tiny visual bounds of the SVG icon. The button itself should have an appropriately sized interactive area.

## Existing functionality must remain intact

Do not break or remove any working feature while implementing the theme system.

Verify that the following still work after the change:

- Home page tool search
- Tool navigation
- Calculator arithmetic
- Scientific calculator functions
- Calculator keyboard controls
- Memory controls
- Calculation history
- Persistent calculator data
- Exporting Elowen data
- Importing Elowen data
- Clearing local data
- Privacy page
- Terms page
- Custom 404 page
- GitHub Pages paths
- Responsive layouts
- PWA/offline functionality if currently implemented

If Elowen has a service worker/cache, update cache versioning if necessary so users receive the new themed assets instead of remaining stuck on old cached CSS or JavaScript.

## Implementation quality

Keep the implementation maintainable.

Prefer one shared theme system used across the entire site rather than separate page-specific theme scripts.

Avoid:

- Copy-pasted theme code on every page
- Inline styles for large parts of the theme
- `!important` everywhere
- Rewriting unrelated working code
- Introducing a framework solely for dark mode
- Adding external icon libraries solely for two icons
- Adding trackers, analytics, APIs, accounts, or backend services

The website must remain static and compatible with GitHub Pages.

## Final verification

Before finishing, test both light and dark themes across the entire existing site.

Confirm all of the following:

- Light mode renders correctly
- Dark mode renders correctly
- Sun icon appears for light mode
- Crescent moon icon appears for dark mode
- Icons are SVG/CSS-based and not emoji
- Theme changes immediately
- Theme survives refresh
- Theme survives navigation
- Initial system preference is respected when the user has no saved choice
- Explicit user choice overrides system preference
- No obvious wrong-theme flash occurs on page load
- Existing backups without theme data still import correctly
- New backups preserve theme preference where appropriate
- No unreadable text exists in either theme
- No component remains accidentally styled for only one theme
- Hover, active, disabled, error, and focus states work in both themes
- Mobile layouts remain correct
- Desktop layouts remain correct
- No horizontal overflow is introduced
- No existing Elowen functionality is broken
- No console errors are introduced

The result should feel native to Elowen: warm, minimal, polished, professional, and consistent in both light and dark environments.
